om the Sanger Institute where they  demonstrate that it is possible to get  information about the genome and the  transcriptome from the same single cell  and in this particular study pacbio  sequencing was used again in this  complementary fashion that I mentioned  at the end of my talk to not only get  counts but also get isoforms different  transcript isoforms for this particular  signal sell these were in cancer cells  and so i think it's a it's a first  demonstration that i know of where the  technology can also be useful for  highlighting differential splicing and  changes in the transcriptome  architecture at the level of single  cells  okay we'll give you another few moments  to send in questions which you can send  them via the green Q&amp;A button at the  lower left of the presentation window do  you have any more questions okay  transcription you see I think it would  be is transcription affected by  environmental factors so how do you  evaluate or control those factors in  sequencing data so I would say that this  is probably done most prominently  through the study design you know the  sequencing will give you the underlying  information about the genome the  transcriptome and certainly the  environmental factors I think are also  known to affect the epigenome and you  know we didn't have time today to talk  about the unique capability of practice  sequencing to characterize the  methylation status of the DNA as a  process of sequencing but it is  certainly true that if you design your  experiment properly and have you know  cases and controls for the different  environmental factors and the sequencing  can give you information about how these  environmental factors change either the  genome Bobby zebra at Mount Sinai  presented a study at AG BTW that was  recorded so he go to our website and  search for the AG BTW workshop he gave  an example where he irradiated cancer  cells and then with the sequel system  and prepared by sequencing looked at the  changes in the structural break points  at particular genes in particular the RV  one gene and identified new structural  break points and hotspots in break  points through this irradiation of the  cancer cells and so this is a true for  the genome level the transcriptome level  and the epigenome level so with a  properly designed study the sequencing  data can give you very important  information on how that changes on the  genomic constitution of the samples see  what is your opinion about the future  sequence methods that can be used so i  would say that at a high at a general  level in high level we've seen a  transition from sequencing being  sequencing and by that I mean that  before the next generation sequencing  and the third generation sequencing  technologies came along there was really  only one method and that was saying or  sequencing and the research community  had to adapt their particular research  projects to of what Sanger sequencing  could provide this is no longer the case  we now have quite a wide variety of  different sequencing technologies and  they have different strengths and  weaknesses and I was trying to point  those out during my presentation that  the alumina technology is extremely good  for counting because you get lots and  lots of reeds and so it you can count on  whatever is in your sample really well  however their limitations with regard to  read length with regard to bias so that  other applications like the Nobel  assembly like resolving some of these  repeat expansion diseases like resolving  full-length transcripts is not very well  suited with short read sequencing and so  that's where pacaya has a strength and  of course on the flip side you know we  don't have the billions of reeds and so  it's not a very good counting tool so  what that means is that the researchers  now have choices and depending on what  they're trying to achieve and the  questions they're trying to address they  have different choices of the different 